In the United States, voter turnout is measured as a percentage, calculated by dividing the total number of votes cast by the voting age population (VAP) or, more recently, the voting eligible population (VEP). Voter turnout in the US has varied over time, between states, and between demographic groups. Turnout is higher for US presidential elections than for midterm elections. Voter turnout is generally lower in the United States than in other advanced democracies.
